  "findings": [
    {
      "id": "A17",
      "statement": "The paper's CAPRISE throughput (2339 vectors/s at d=768 = 428 us/vector) is consistent with a per-element Python implementation (~0.56 us/element); this reproduction uses vectorised numpy noise generation (one PCG64 call for all d Gaussians) and reaches 86492 embeddings/s at d=768 (~37x the paper's implementation). Absolute numbers are therefore implementation-style sensitive, not scheme-inherent."
    },
    {
      "id": "A17b",
      "statement": "PHE baseline: Paillier encryption is ~2-3 orders of magnitude slower than CAPRISE here (measured python-phe + GMP; numbers depend on key size 1024/2048-bit and implementation), but RemoteRAG's reported 250-300 vectors/s corresponds to an optimised C/GPU implementation on A100; cross-hardware/cross-implementation speedup ratios must be interpreted qualitatively. Within the paper's own numbers 2339/250-300 = 7.8-9.4x ('9x') is internally consistent."
    },
    {
      "id": "A17c",
      "statement": "Phase-1: the embedding step dominates. On this machine gtr-t5-base embeds 128 queries in ~224 ms (MPS fp16) / ~606 ms (CPU fp32), while CAPRISE encrypts the same batch in ~1.6 ms, so the encryption overhead is ~0.26-0.71% (paper: 15/79.52 = 18.9%, <19%). CAPRISE is even relatively cheaper here because our implementation is faster; the paper's 15 ms encryption corresponds to a per-element Python implementation. Embedding remains the bottleneck in both."
    }
  ],
  "runtime_s": 28.3
